# 0098-choose-which-games-shaders-are-prepared.patch -- the owner's request W: Mario Kart's ~60,000 libraries kept South Park's shader preparation from ever getting its turn; the person
# chooses the order. Applies after 0097. UNTESTED ON DEVICE AND IN THE SIMULATOR: compiled, and its pure part ran in the host harness (below).
#
# W. WarmOrder (GameAppField.cs, pure) over the warm queue (GameAppWarm.cs: WarmQueue gains Held and Skipped):
#    - The Build tab's SHADERS section lists the games in their order, then the skipped ones: "Mario Kart 8 Deluxe · 12,400 of 60,112" (being prepared, live), "South Park · waiting · 5,009"
#      (its libraries; "1,200 of 5,009" once begun: its warmed.tsv rows for this app build and chain over its pack's .metal entries, read on the sync's worker, the image's count kept while
#      the image is the same file), "· paused", "· skipped". Each game has a "…" menu: "Prepare this one now", "Pause" / "Resume" (that game only: it keeps its place and is passed over),
#      "Skip" (left unprepared, out of the queue; "Queue again" puts it back at the end). The game's sheet: "Prepare this one now", "Pause / Resume this game's preparation", "Skip its
#      preparation" / "Queue its preparation again" (values "Paused (this game)", "Skipped"). Its long-press menu: "Prepare its shaders now" (a skipped game too).
#    - "Prepare this one now": the game first (un-paused, un-skipped), saved at once; the game being prepared stops at its next library (StopWarm; what it finished is in its warmed.tsv and
#      is skipped when its turn comes back: it RESUMES, it does not restart); the chosen game starts (WarmSync -> WarmKick); one log line: "shader warm-up: South Park moved first by you
#      (Mario Kart 8 Deluxe paused at 12,400 of 60,112)". Never two runs at once (WarmOrder.Next: none while one runs, while everything is paused or something else has the phone;
#      GameWarm's one-run rule of 0087/0093 stays).
#    - The order persists in gameapp.warmqueue.tsv ("held" and "skipped" rows beside "paused" and "title"; an older app reads it and ignores them); a game the person put first stays first; a
#      game newly built goes to the END (a finished build put its game first before: PrepareAfterBuild no longer passes it as the front); a game that owes nothing any more (prepared,
#      deleted) leaves and is forgotten as paused or skipped.
#    - The lock screen / Dynamic Island: the warm-up's background task is retargeted to the game being prepared at every start (BackgroundRetargetWarm, as before).
#
# VERIFIED (2026-11-22, M1 Pro): check-patches-apply.sh: every patch 0010-0098 applies in order, no fuzz, no offset; the repository's patches give exactly the compiled tree. Compile
#   (-t:Compile): 0 errors / 40 warnings, the same per file and code as before 0080. Host harness (harness98: … P97;P98): 387 of 387 checks (19 new: a new game at the end, move first, the
#   order kept, per-game pause, no double run, skip / queue again, the file's round trip and an older file, the list's words, the switch line, resume not restart, the lock screen, the
#   wiring); 11 of 11 mutations killed.
# NOT VERIFIED: nothing ran on a device: a real switch from a 60,000-library run (the stop comes at the run's next library), the list's look, the counts of a real pack.
# NEW UI STRINGS: "Prepare this one now", "Prepare its shaders now", "Pause", "Resume", "Skip", "Left unprepared; it can be queued again", "Queue again", "Pause this game's preparation",
#   "Resume this game's preparation", "Skip its preparation", "Queue its preparation again", "Paused (this game)", "Skipped"; log lines " moved first by you", "(it waits at the end of the
#   queue)", "paused by you (it keeps its place; what it finished is kept)", "skipped by you: left unprepared (Queue again puts it back)", "queued again by you, at the end".
